time—about 50 million billion years.)
3. Consider the following recursive function:
int power( int x, int n ) {
// Compute x raised to the power n.
// Assume that n >= 0.
int answer;
if ( n == 0 ) {
answer = 1;
}
else if (n % 2 == 0) {
answer = power( x * x, n / 2);
}
else {
answer = x * power( x * x, (n-1) / 2);
}
return answer;
}
Show that for any integer x and any non-negative integer n, the function power (x, n)
correctly computes the value of x
n
. (Assume that the int data type can represent
arbitrarily large integers.) Note that the test “ if (n % 2 == 0) ” tests whether n
is evenly divisible by 2. That is, the test is true if n is an even number. (This
function is actually a very efficient way to compute x
n
.)
4. A leaf node in a binary tree is a node in which both the left and the right subtrees
are empty. Prove that the following recursive function correctly counts the number
of leaves in a binary tree:
int LeafCount( BinaryTreeNode root ) {
// Counts the number of leaf nodes in
// the tree with the specified root.
int count;
if ( root == null ) {
count = 0;
}
else if ( root.left == null && root.right == null ) {
count = 1;
}
else {
count = LeafCount( root.left );
count = count + LeafCount( root.right );
}
return count;
}

